id: cover
name: Cover
kind: battlefield
source: Core Rule
description: >
  A Feature between you and whoever is shooting at you. Dodge 2(T) better, and take twice
  its Hardness Value less Damage.
text: >
  Cover. If a Feature on an Adjacent Square to you is between you and an Opponent that is
  targeting you with an Attacking Maneuver, increase your Dodge Roll by 2(T) for the
  duration of that Attacking Maneuver and reduce the Damage you would suffer by twice the
  Hardness Value of that Feature. This effect does not apply if the Attacking Maneuver
  possesses an AoE that also includes the Feature as a target.

---

# --- Turned on by hand -------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "A Feature on an Adjacent Square to you is between you and an Opponent." Three things
# this system has none of: Squares, Features, and any notion of what is between two
# people. So nothing works this out - the player says whether they are behind something,
# and says how hard it is.
#
# Which is the whole of the Battlefield section's shape: the rules are about one character,
# and what the map looks like is the table's.
#
# The toggle is what decides whether this file runs at all. There is no condition inside
# it: a Trait that is on you applies, and one that is not is not gathered - the same way a
# Combat Condition works. Writing the test into the script as well would be one rule in two
# places.
#
# --- The Dodge ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Increase your Dodge Roll by 2(T) for the duration of that Attacking Maneuver." (T), the
# Tier of Power, which is what an unmarked (T) means everywhere here.
#
# A standing bonus while the toggle is on, because a duration this system cannot see the
# start or the end of is a duration the player holds. Turn it on for the attack and off
# afterwards; the sheet says it is on.
#
# --- The Damage -------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "Reduce the Damage you would suffer by twice the Hardness Value of that Feature."
#
# Written to `incoming.damage` rather than to Damage Reduction, and the difference is not
# cosmetic: Damage Reduction is a stat of yours that a Profile can pierce or halve, and
# this is a wall taking the hit instead of you. A Concentrated attack ignoring half your
# Damage Reduction has nothing to say about the rock you are standing behind.
#
# Which is also why it is an automatic block on `before-wound` rather than a passive: that
# is the moment the Damage this reduces exists, and it is the moment Broken's own
# `incoming.damage` answers.
#
# "Twice the Hardness Value" - the Value, which is the number the player types. The entry
# also names a Hardness Rank, and that is the Feature's Life Points rather than this.
#
# --- What is not built --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# "This effect does not apply if the Attacking Maneuver possesses an AoE that also includes
# the Feature as a target." An Area of Effect is a shape over Squares, and a Feature is a
# thing in them - neither exists here. So the exception is the table's to spot, and the
# answer is to turn the toggle off for that attack.
#
# Said on the sheet beside the toggle, where somebody deciding whether to leave it on is
# looking.

[passive]
dodge += 2(T);

[automatic/before-wound]
incoming.damage -= 2 * battlefield.cover.hardness;
